WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.705
An adversarial example is supposed to be a small
change that does not alter the true category of the input.

00:00:06.785 --> 00:00:09.622
For images a human can verify this by looking.

00:00:09.702 --> 00:00:12.222
For graphs there is no such visual check,

00:00:12.302 --> 00:00:16.787
so the community settled on counting edited edges
with an ℓ-zero budget.

00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:19.958
But real graphs are dominated by low-degree nodes,

00:00:20.038 --> 00:00:25.284
and for those nodes even a tiny edge budget can
completely rewire the neighbourhood.

00:00:25.364 --> 00:00:31.181
That raises a fundamental and previously unanswered
question: do these standard perturbations

00:00:31.261 --> 00:00:34.225
really keep a node's semantic content unchanged,

00:00:34.305 --> 00:00:38.220
or are we attacking nodes whose true label has
already flipped?
